Native 60 FPS for SCUS-94154: open the game's own 30 Hz frame gate, reuse the
engine's existing frame-time compensation, give the guest CPU the headroom a
60 Hz frame needs, and close the gate again whenever the machine cannot
actually sustain it. Present in BOTH builds - the release runtime is the one
players run - and driven by PSX_CRASH2_60FPS from the launcher setting.

New runtime/src/crash2_60fps.h carries the full reasoning. In short:
func_80016F04 already snaps each measured frame time to whole NTSC fields and
the motion sites multiply by it, so a 60 Hz loop halves per-frame displacement
instead of doubling world speed - but a scene that sits ON the one-field
boundary alternates between one and two fields, which measured 45-50 loops/s
in Turtle Woods and looked worse than the 30 the game locks itself to. The
sustain guard gives those scenes their own lock back.

psx_cycles.c also loses the two 64-bit divisions it did per cycle charge. They
were affordable for short debugtools measurements and are not affordable in a
player build whose whole problem is host throughput.

--- /dev/null
+++ b/runtime/src/crash2_60fps.h
@@ -0,0 +1,369 @@
+/* SCUS-94154 native 60 FPS: open the game's own 30 Hz frame gate, and close it
+ * again whenever the machine cannot actually sustain it.
+ *
+ * Crash 2 ends a frame in func_8001658C. It always waits one VSync(0) at
+ * 0x80016838, then compares the previous frame's duration against 25 at
+ * 0x8001685C and waits a SECOND VSync(0) at 0x80016868 when it was shorter.
+ * Its time unit is one period of root counter 2, which 0x800156A0 programs
+ * with target 0x1000 off the system clock / 8: 4233600 / 4096 = 1033.6 Hz, so
+ * one NTSC field is ~17.24 ticks. The comparison against 25 therefore means
+ * "round this frame up to two fields", and that second wait - only that one -
+ * is what pins the game to 30 Hz.
+ *
+ * Opening the gate does NOT double game speed, because the engine already
+ * scales motion by measured time. func_80016F04 snaps the last frame's tick
+ * count to a whole number of fields (< 19 -> 17, < 36 -> 34, < 53 -> 51) and
+ * 0x8001697C stores it in the draw buffer at +20. Motion call sites read it
+ * back - 0x8001D400, 0x8001D9D4 and 0x8001DADC all do the same
+ * clamp-to-102 then (velocity * field_ticks) >> 10. At 60 Hz that field count
+ * becomes 17 instead of 34 and per-frame displacement halves. This is the
+ * compensation the original design already carries for dropped frames; we are
+ * reusing it, not inventing one.
+ *
+ * The FIRST wait stays, so a frame whose guest work does not fit in one field
+ * lands on the next VBlank and the game falls back to 30 (or 20) Hz exactly as
+ * it does on hardware, with the field count following it down. That covers the
+ * GUEST running out of budget.
+ *
+ * Two things it does NOT cover, both measured in play, and both of which made
+ * this mode worse than the 30 Hz lock it removes:
+ *
+ *   - the HOST running out. Then guest time dilates instead: VBlank itself
+ *     arrives late and the whole machine, audio included, runs slow.
+ *   - the scene sitting ON the one-field boundary, so frames alternate between
+ *     one field and two. Presentation alternates 16.7/33.4 ms, the engine's
+ *     compensation reads the PREVIOUS frame's field count and so lands a frame
+ *     late, and the average is a number no display period divides. Turtle
+ *     Woods measured 45-50 loops/s this way, with dips below 30; Snow Go on
+ *     the same machine held 55-56 and was fine.
+ *
+ * So the gate is not a one-way switch. Every second c2_60_window_verdict looks
+ * at both, and on a failure this steps back down - for a host failure by
+ * dropping the CPU headroom first, since that is the cheaper half, otherwise
+ * by closing the gate and handing the game back its own lock. It retries later
+ * with a growing delay, and a sustained good run clears the penalty. The point
+ * is that a scene the machine cannot hold at 60 gets a clean 30 rather than a
+ * ragged 47.
+ *
+ * Guest budget is a separate, real limit. At the Turtle Woods crates the outer
+ * loop needed a p95 of 672,072 guest cycles where one NTSC field affords
+ * 564,480, so the mode can also raise the CPU-only clock (see psx_cycles.c),
+ * leaving VBlank, CD, SPU and the timers - including the root counter this
+ * engine measures time with - on their original rate. That costs host
+ * throughput, which is why the guard drops it first.
+ *
+ * Everything here is reversible and signature-guarded: nothing is written
+ * unless all six anchor words of this exact revision read back correctly.
+ */
+#ifndef CRASH2_60FPS_H
+#define CRASH2_60FPS_H
+
+#include <stdlib.h>
+
+#define C2_60_LOOP_PC    0x80011848u
+#define C2_60_GATE_PC    0x8001685Cu
+#define C2_60_GATE_ORIG  0x2C630019u   /* sltiu v1,v1,25  - the 30 Hz gate */
+#define C2_60_GATE_OPEN  0x24030000u   /* addiu v1,zero,0 - never wait twice */
+#define C2_60_DB_CUR     0x80063450u   /* draw buffer shown last; +20 = ticks */
+#define C2_60_TICKS      0x8003BEA4u   /* root-counter-2 tick accumulator */
+/* The engine's timing mode. 0 is live play; the game writes 2 when it starts
+ * demo PLAYBACK (0x8002FD10, which sets up the recorded stream right after),
+ * 3 for demo RECORDING (0x8002FF34) and 4 when the demo ends (0x80015D60),
+ * and back to 0 at 0x80015D90, 0x8001F9EC and 0x8002FCDC. In playback the
+ * clock is taken from the recording itself (0x80016940) rather than from the
+ * root counter, so how fast the loop runs is how fast the recorded timeline is
+ * consumed - which is not something this mode should be deciding. */
+#define C2_60_TIMING     0x8006CD14u
+
+/* One second is long enough to average out a single slow frame and short
+ * enough that a bad scene is not endured. 57 of the 59.94 VBlanks a second is
+ * ~95% speed: below that the slowdown is audible, above it nobody can tell. */
+#define C2_60_WINDOW_MS   1000u
+#define C2_60_MIN_VBLANK  57u
+/* Percentage of the window's frames the engine must have simulated as ONE
+ * field. Measured: Snow Go holds 55-56 loops/s, which is ~93% of frames on 17
+ * ticks; a Turtle Woods route averaging 48.49 loops/s at 1.236 VBlanks per new
+ * image is ~76%. 85 sits between the two and puts the line near 52 loops/s.
+ * Anything below it is the alternating regime this exists to reject - see the
+ * comment on c2_60_window_verdict. */
+#define C2_60_MIN_HOLD    85u
+/* Windows with fewer loops than this are not judged on hold at all: a load or
+ * 2D screen legitimately updates ~8 times a second and is not a failure. */
+#define C2_60_MIN_LOOPS   20u
+#define C2_60_RETRY_MS    8000u    /* doubles per consecutive failure */
+#define C2_60_RETRY_MAX   64000u
+#define C2_60_CLEAR_MS    10000u   /* sustained 60 that forgives past failures */
+
+/* Verdicts from one window. */
+#define C2_60_PENDING     (-1)
+#define C2_60_OK          0
+#define C2_60_FAIL_HOST   1        /* emulator behind the wall clock */
+#define C2_60_FAIL_HOLD   2        /* scene cannot stay on one field */
+
+static int c2_60_mode = -1;            /* -1 not read yet, 0 off, 1 on */
+static int c2_60_cpu_pct = 125;        /* what the setting asked for */
+static int c2_60_cpu_now = 100;        /* what the guard is currently using */
+static int c2_60_verified = -1;        /* -1 unknown, 0 mismatch, 1 confirmed */
+static int c2_60_gate_open;
+static int c2_60_suspended;            /* held closed by the timing mode */
+static unsigned c2_60_timing_mode;
+static int c2_60_fails;                /* consecutive back-offs */
+static int c2_60_hold_pct = C2_60_MIN_HOLD;
+static unsigned long long c2_60_opened, c2_60_backoffs;
+
+/* Sustain window. Host milliseconds and the VBlank total are both read on the
+ * emulation thread, once per outer loop. */
+static unsigned long long c2_60_win_ms, c2_60_win_vbl, c2_60_good_ms;
+static unsigned long long c2_60_retry_at;
+static unsigned c2_60_win_loops, c2_60_win_one_field;
+static unsigned c2_60_loop_hz, c2_60_hold_seen;
+
+/* Sampled once per outer loop so the release heartbeat thread can report them
+ * without dereferencing a guest pointer the game may be rewriting underneath
+ * it. */
+static unsigned c2_60_last_frame_ticks;
+static unsigned c2_60_last_guest_ticks;
+
+/* The quantizer and both VSync call sites, not just the gate word: the gate is
+ * only safe to remove while the engine's own frame-time compensation is the
+ * one documented above. */
+static int c2_60_signature_ok(CPUState *cpu)
+{
+    return cpu->read_word(0x80016838u) == 0x0C012921u &&  /* jal VSync */
+           cpu->read_word(0x8001683Cu) == 0x00002021u &&  /* a0 = 0 */
+           cpu->read_word(0x80016860u) == 0x10600003u &&  /* beq v1,zero,+3 */
+           cpu->read_word(0x80016868u) == 0x0C012921u &&  /* jal VSync */
+           cpu->read_word(0x80016F04u) == 0x04800008u &&  /* quantizer entry */
+           cpu->read_word(0x80016F18u) == 0x34040011u;    /* ori a0,zero,17 */
+}
+
+static uint32_t c2_60_read_ram_word(uint32_t address)
+{
+    extern uint32_t psx_read_word(uint32_t address);
+    if (address < 0x80000000u || address > 0x801FFFFCu || (address & 3u))
+        return 0u;
+    return psx_read_word(address);
+}
+
+static void c2_60_set_cpu(int percent)
+{
+    extern void psx_crash2_cpu_clock_set(uint32_t percent);
+    if (percent == c2_60_cpu_now) return;
+    c2_60_cpu_now = percent;
+    psx_crash2_cpu_clock_set((uint32_t)percent);
+}
+
+static void c2_60_close_gate(void)
+{
+    extern void psx_mod_write_code_word(uint32_t address, uint32_t value);
+    if (c2_60_read_ram_word(C2_60_GATE_PC) == C2_60_GATE_OPEN)
+        psx_mod_write_code_word(C2_60_GATE_PC, C2_60_GATE_ORIG);
+    c2_60_gate_open = 0;
+    c2_60_set_cpu(100);
+}
+
+/* The engine's own opinion of how long the last frame took, in 1/1033.6 s
+ * ticks snapped to whole NTSC fields. 17 means it simulated a 60 Hz step, 34 a
+ * 30 Hz one. This is the number motion is multiplied by, so it - not the host
+ * swap rate - is what says whether the game is really running at 60. */
+unsigned crash2_60fps_frame_ticks(void) { return c2_60_last_frame_ticks; }
+
+/* Free-running guest tick count. Must stay near 1033.6/s in every mode: if a
+ * CPU-clock change ever moved it, the engine's time base would have moved too
+ * and the compensation above would be measuring the wrong second. */
+unsigned crash2_60fps_guest_ticks(void) { return c2_60_last_guest_ticks; }
+
+int crash2_60fps_mode(void) { return c2_60_mode < 0 ? 0 : c2_60_mode; }
+int crash2_60fps_verified(void) { return c2_60_verified; }
+int crash2_60fps_cpu_percent(void) { return c2_60_cpu_now; }
+int crash2_60fps_gate_open(void) { return c2_60_gate_open; }
+unsigned crash2_60fps_loop_hz(void) { return c2_60_loop_hz; }
+/* Percentage of the last window's frames the engine simulated as one field.
+ * This is the number the hold decision is made on, so report it: a scene that
+ * keeps falling back is answering with it. */
+unsigned crash2_60fps_hold_pct(void) { return c2_60_hold_seen; }
+/* Non-zero while the gate is held closed because the game is recording or
+ * replaying a demo, rather than because the scene could not hold 60. The two
+ * are not the same thing and a report that conflated them would send someone
+ * chasing performance in an attract loop. */
+unsigned crash2_60fps_suspended(void) { return c2_60_suspended ? c2_60_timing_mode : 0u; }
+unsigned long long crash2_60fps_open_count(void) { return c2_60_opened; }
+unsigned long long crash2_60fps_backoff_count(void) { return c2_60_backoffs; }
+
+/* Live toggle for the debug server. Turning it off restores the original
+ * comparison and the original CPU clock in the same call, so an aborted
+ * experiment cannot leave a patched word behind. */
+void crash2_60fps_set_mode(int on)
+{
+    c2_60_mode = on ? 1 : 0;
+    if (!on) {
+        c2_60_close_gate();
+    } else {
+        /* A deliberate switch-on clears the guard's penalty: the player asked
+         * again, and the scene may well be a different one. */
+        c2_60_fails = 0;
+        c2_60_retry_at = 0;
+        c2_60_win_ms = 0;
+    }
+}
+
+void crash2_60fps_set_cpu_percent(int percent)
+{
+    if (percent < 100 || percent > 150) return;
+    c2_60_cpu_pct = percent;
+    if (c2_60_gate_open) c2_60_set_cpu(percent);
+}
+
+/* Judge one window. Two different things can go wrong, and they need different
+ * answers.
+ *
+ * FAIL_HOST is guest time falling behind the wall clock: the emulator cannot
+ * keep up, so VBlank itself arrives late and the whole machine - audio
+ * included - runs slow.
+ *
+ * FAIL_HOLD is the regime that actually made this mode unpleasant. With the
+ * gate open, a frame takes one field if its work fits and two if it does not,
+ * and a scene sitting near that boundary alternates. Three things then go
+ * wrong at once: presentation alternates 16.7/33.4 ms, the engine's
+ * compensation reads the PREVIOUS frame's field count so the scale lands a
+ * frame late, and the average is a number like 45-50 that no display period
+ * divides. Measured in Turtle Woods, and it is worse to watch than the clean
+ * 30 the game locks itself to. So a scene that cannot mostly stay on one field
+ * is given back its own lock.
+ *
+ * A window with few loops is not judged on hold: a load or 2D screen updates
+ * a handful of times a second by design and is not a failing scene. */
+static int c2_60_window_verdict(unsigned long long now, unsigned long long vbl)
+{
+    if (c2_60_win_ms == 0) {
+        c2_60_win_ms = now;
+        c2_60_win_vbl = vbl;
+        c2_60_win_loops = c2_60_win_one_field = 0;
+        return C2_60_PENDING;
+    }
+    unsigned long long span = now - c2_60_win_ms;
+    if (span < C2_60_WINDOW_MS) return C2_60_PENDING;
+    const unsigned long long vblanks = vbl - c2_60_win_vbl;
+    const unsigned loops = c2_60_win_loops, one_field = c2_60_win_one_field;
+    c2_60_loop_hz = (unsigned)((unsigned long long)loops * 1000u / span);
+    c2_60_hold_seen = loops ? (unsigned)((unsigned long long)one_field * 100u / loops) : 0u;
+    c2_60_win_ms = now;
+    c2_60_win_vbl = vbl;
+    c2_60_win_loops = c2_60_win_one_field = 0;
+
+    if (vblanks * 1000u < (unsigned long long)C2_60_MIN_VBLANK * span)
+        return C2_60_FAIL_HOST;
+    if (c2_60_hold_pct > 0 && loops >= C2_60_MIN_LOOPS
+        && one_field * 100u < (unsigned)c2_60_hold_pct * loops)
+        return C2_60_FAIL_HOLD;
+    return C2_60_OK;
+}
+
+/* Called at the outer game loop, so at most once per simulated frame: the six
+ * signature reads cost nothing at 30-60 Hz and re-checking every loop is what
+ * makes this survive the title reloading its own executable. */
+static void crash2_60fps(CPUState *cpu, uint32_t pc)
+{
+    extern uint64_t g_vblank_raise_count;
+    extern uint64_t psx_host_mono_ms(void);
+
+    if (pc != C2_60_LOOP_PC) return;
+
+    /* Sampled in every mode: at stock these read 34 and ~1033.6/s, which is
+     * what a 60 FPS run has to be compared against. */
+    c2_60_last_frame_ticks =
+        (unsigned)c2_60_read_ram_word(c2_60_read_ram_word(C2_60_DB_CUR) + 20u);
+    c2_60_last_guest_ticks = (unsigned)c2_60_read_ram_word(C2_60_TICKS);
+    c2_60_timing_mode = (unsigned)c2_60_read_ram_word(C2_60_TIMING);
+
+    if (c2_60_mode < 0) {
+        const char *e = getenv("PSX_CRASH2_60FPS");
+        const char *p = getenv("PSX_CRASH2_60FPS_CPU_PCT");
+        const char *h = getenv("PSX_CRASH2_60FPS_HOLD_PCT");
+        c2_60_mode = (e && *e == '1') ? 1 : 0;
+        if (p) {
+            int want = atoi(p);
+            if (want >= 100 && want <= 150) c2_60_cpu_pct = want;
+        }
+        /* Escape hatch, deliberately not in the launcher UI. 0 disables the
+         * hold check, which is how to get the ragged 45-50 back if someone
+         * genuinely prefers it to a clean 30. */
+        if (h) {
+            int want = atoi(h);
+            if (want >= 0 && want <= 100) c2_60_hold_pct = want;
+        }
+    }
+    if (c2_60_mode != 1) return;
+
+    const unsigned long long now = psx_host_mono_ms();
+    c2_60_win_loops++;
+    if (c2_60_last_frame_ticks == 17u) c2_60_win_one_field++;
+    /* The guest can revert the word itself by reloading its executable. */
+    if (c2_60_gate_open && cpu->read_word(C2_60_GATE_PC) != C2_60_GATE_OPEN)
+        c2_60_gate_open = 0;
+
+    const int verdict = c2_60_window_verdict(now, g_vblank_raise_count);
+
+    /* Demo record/playback: suspend rather than back off. Nothing is wrong
+     * with the scene, so this earns no penalty and no retry delay - the gate
+     * comes straight back when live play resumes. */
+    if (c2_60_timing_mode != 0u) {
+        if (c2_60_gate_open) {
+            c2_60_close_gate();
+            c2_60_suspended = 1;
+            c2_60_good_ms = 0;
+            c2_60_win_ms = 0;
+        }
+        return;
+    }
+    c2_60_suspended = 0;
+
+    if (c2_60_gate_open) {
+        if (verdict == C2_60_FAIL_HOST && c2_60_cpu_now > 100) {
+            /* Cheapest step first: the headroom is what costs the host the
+             * extra guest instructions. Heavy scenes then fall back to 30
+             * through the game's own first VSync wait, which is fine. Hold
+             * failures are not treated this way - more headroom is what got
+             * the scene to the boundary it is now oscillating around. */
+            c2_60_set_cpu(100);
+            c2_60_good_ms = 0;
+        } else if (verdict == C2_60_FAIL_HOST || verdict == C2_60_FAIL_HOLD) {
+            c2_60_close_gate();
+            c2_60_backoffs++;
+            unsigned long long wait = C2_60_RETRY_MS << (c2_60_fails < 3 ? c2_60_fails : 3);
+            if (wait > C2_60_RETRY_MAX) wait = C2_60_RETRY_MAX;
+            c2_60_retry_at = now + wait;
+            if (c2_60_fails < 16) c2_60_fails++;
+            c2_60_good_ms = 0;
+        } else if (verdict == C2_60_OK) {
+            c2_60_good_ms += C2_60_WINDOW_MS;
+            if (c2_60_good_ms >= C2_60_CLEAR_MS) {
+                c2_60_fails = 0;
+                /* Earned the headroom back, if the setting asked for it. */
+                c2_60_set_cpu(c2_60_cpu_pct);
+                c2_60_good_ms = 0;
+            }
+        }
+        return;
+    }
+
+    if (now < c2_60_retry_at) return;
+
+    uint32_t gate = cpu->read_word(C2_60_GATE_PC);
+    if (gate != C2_60_GATE_ORIG || !c2_60_signature_ok(cpu)) {
+        c2_60_verified = 0;
+        return;
+    }
+    {
+        extern void psx_mod_write_code_word(uint32_t address, uint32_t value);
+        c2_60_verified = 1;
+        psx_mod_write_code_word(C2_60_GATE_PC, C2_60_GATE_OPEN);
+        c2_60_set_cpu(c2_60_cpu_pct);
+        c2_60_gate_open = 1;
+        c2_60_good_ms = 0;
+        c2_60_win_ms = 0;          /* do not judge the transition window */
+        c2_60_opened++;
+    }
+}
+
+#endif /* CRASH2_60FPS_H */
